>
  <p>Sleep is where recovery happens, and strength coach Jordan Hoppel puts it near the top of the list for anyone who trains.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Get as much sleep as you possibly can. More sleep can only benefit your fitness goals.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>Jordan's tips for bad sleepers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Get into a routine.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Mind the temperature.</strong> Keep the air low and crisp.</li>
    <li><strong>Take a hot shower at night.</strong> The body cools itself afterward, which is good for sleep.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid lots of lights,</strong> and stay away from screens and bright lights.</li>
    <li><strong>Eat enough calories and protein during the day</strong> so you don't get hunger pangs at night.</li>
    <li><strong>Lower stress at the end of the day.</strong> Get into a parasympathetic state where the brain isn't actively stressing. Reading a book can help.</li>
    <li><strong>Supplements</strong> such as magnesium, melatonin, and GABA can also help.</li>
  </ul>

  <Media type="image" alt="Dark, cool bedroom set up for sleep with a book on the nightstand" caption="Cool, dark, and no screens." brief="Photo or illustration of a cool, dark bedroom with a book." />

  <h2>Caffeine and pre-workout</h2>
  <p>If you want to take it and you feel good when you take it, take it. But if it affects your sleep, don't take it for the workout. Make sure any caffeine or pre-workout isn't affecting your sleep.</p>

  <h2>Sleep and deloads</h2>
  <p>Sleep and training load are linked: poor sleep and a workout that feels like a grind are two of the signs Jordan watches for before he calls a <a href="/what-is-a-deload-week/">deload</a>.</p>
